00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.370
Hey, David, why are the most dangerous cities
run by Democrats?

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.320
So this is a pretty old trope.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.389
And I maybe we can kind of address this piece
by piece.

00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:18.039
Donald Trump and Republicans have been harping
on this a lot.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:20.740
Democrat run cities are riddled with violence.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.260
So vote for me instead of Joe Biden, even
though, of course, Trump is already president.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.260
And there's still violence in

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:27.260
those cities.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.259
So here's what there's a few things you have
to understand.

00:00:30.259 --> 00:00:32.060
Most cities are run by Democrats.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.060
Period.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.970
So violent cities are run by Democrats and
nonviolent cities are run by Democrats to

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:38.970
use crass terminology.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:44.949
Additionally, the violence is disproportionately
in urban areas.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:51.260
So when you think about cities instead of
areas, you start to get this talking point

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.690
that Trump has been repeated.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:01:00.590
So if you look at mayors, 35 of the 50 largest
cities are run by Democrats.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.570
13 are run by Republicans.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.450
And two are Democratic leaning independents.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:13.410
So because more cities are run by Democrats,
you have to account for that in your analysis.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:20.480
The large Republican cities are San Antonio,
I'm sorry, San Diego, run by Republican mayor,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.500
San Diego, Jacksonville.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.610
Jacksonville is more dangerous than the average
in Florida.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.240
It's run by a Republican.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.280
Fort Worth, Texas, run by a Republican.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.130
Is it more dangerous than the average of Texas
in terms of crime rates?

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.390
El Paso, Texas, is safer than average for
Texas.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.670
Oklahoma City run by a Republican.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.480
More dangerous than average.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.740
So what you really just have to understand
is that there is a sort of base rate bias

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.440
that you do you have to account for.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.470
The largest cities run by Democrats are the
largest cities in the country.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:58.870
Crime tends to be greater in places with large
population density for a number of different

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.090
reasons that we understand cities have more
crime because they're more densely populated.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:07.490
Cities also vote more for Democrats because
they are more cosmopolitan.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.879
Higher education rates, we know more educated
people tend to vote for Democrats instead

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:12.140
of Republicans.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:18.159
And that does not directly linked to the crime
is because the mayor is a Democrat and this

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:19.790
has been widely debunked

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.129
and it doesn't even pass the sniff test.

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:27.370
There is not actually any correlation between
crime and Democrat Democratic mayors when

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.569
you can't account for crime being more prevalent.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:37.620
In cities and the fact that Democrats lead
a majority of the cities in the United States,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.900
including cities with low crime rates.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:46.799
Now when you look at the South, for example,
and the south at both the city and state level

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.760
is disproportionately under Republican rule.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.180
They have some of the highest poverty rates
in the United States.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.489
Does that mean that Republican rule causes
poverty?

00:02:57.489 --> 00:02:58.489
Maybe it does.

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:03.159
And that's a better argument, quite frankly,
than crime and so-called Democrat cities.

00:03:03.159 --> 00:03:07.769
But you still have to look at details and
understand more to really know what it is

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:08.799
that is going on.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:16.030
Now, when this conversation comes up, we also
shouldn't ignore a broader context about crime.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.030
That crime

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:22.959
has been decreasing for decades overall, and
we might be better served understanding that.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.930
And also looking at areas rather than just
cities.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:31.530
What Republicans do is they shift the focus
to cities rather than areas.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.760
And that allows them to play on these bigoted,
racist ideas that the city is filled with

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:42.139
these scary minorities who are going to rob
you and sexually assault you and sort of like

00:03:42.139 --> 00:03:46.120
the Willie Horton ad type of mentality.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:53.430
What that shifts attention away from is the
reality that Republican dominated areas have

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:59.540
violent crime, that on a per capita basis
is just as bad as a lot of the city's cities

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.599
have more people and have more population
